     ppmmccssttaatt — performance measurement with performance monitoring hardware

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppmmccssttaatt [--AA] [--CC] [--DD _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e] [--EE] [--FF _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e] [--GG _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e] [--II]
             [--LL] [--MM _m_a_p_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e] [--NN] [--OO _l_o_g_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e] [--PP _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c]
             [--RR _l_o_g_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e] [--SS _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c] [--TT] [--UU] [--WW] [--aa _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e]
             [--cc _c_p_u_-_s_p_e_c] [--dd] [--ee] [--ff _p_l_u_g_i_n_o_p_t] [--gg] [--ii _l_w_p] [--ll _s_e_c_s]
             [--mm _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e] [--nn _r_a_t_e] [--oo _o_u_t_p_u_t_f_i_l_e] [--pp _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c] [--qq]
             [--rr _f_s_r_o_o_t] [--ss _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c] [--tt _p_r_o_c_e_s_s_-_s_p_e_c] [--uu _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c]
             [--vv] [--ww _s_e_c_s] [--zz _g_r_a_p_h_d_e_p_t_h] [_c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g_s]]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppmmccssttaatt utility measures system performance using the facilities pro‐
     vided by hwpmc(4).

     The ppmmccssttaatt utility can measure both hardware events seen by the system
     as a whole, and those seen when a specified set of processes are execut‐
     ing on the system's CPUs.  If a specific set of processes is being tar‐
     geted (for example, if the --tt _p_r_o_c_e_s_s_-_s_p_e_c option is specified, or if a
     command line is specified using _c_o_m_m_a_n_d), then measurement occurs till
     _c_o_m_m_a_n_d exits, or till all target processes specified by the --tt
     _p_r_o_c_e_s_s_-_s_p_e_c options exit, or till the ppmmccssttaatt utility is interrupted by
     the user.  If a specific set of processes is not targeted for measure‐
     ment, then ppmmccssttaatt will perform system-wide measurements till interrupted
     by the user.

     A given invocation of ppmmccssttaatt can mix allocations of system-mode and
     process-mode PMCs, of both counting and sampling flavors.  The values of
     all counting PMCs are printed in human readable form at regular intervals
     by ppmmccssttaatt.  The format of ppmmccssttaatt's human-readable textual output is not
     stable, and could change in the future.  The output of sampling PMCs may
     be configured to go to a log file for subsequent offline analysis, or, at
     the expense of greater overhead, may be configured to be printed in text
     form on the fly.

     Hardware events to measure are specified to ppmmccssttaatt using event specifier
     strings _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c.  The syntax of these event specifiers is machine de‐
     pendent and is documented in pmc(3).

     A process-mode PMC may be configured to be inheritable by the target
     process' current and future children.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     The following options are available:

     --AA      Skip symbol lookup and display address instead.

     --CC      Toggle between showing cumulative or incremental counts for sub‐
             sequent counting mode PMCs specified on the command line.  The
             default is to show incremental counts.

     --DD _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e
             Create files with per-program samples in the directory named by
             _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e.  The default is to create these files in the current
             directory.

     --EE      Toggle showing per-process counts at the time a tracked process
             exits for subsequent process-mode PMCs specified on the command
             line.  This option is useful for mapping the performance charac‐
             teristics of a complex pipeline of processes when used in con‐
             junction with the --dd option.  The default is to not to enable
             per-process tracking.

     --FF _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e
             Print calltree (Kcachegrind) information to file _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e.  If
             argument _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e is a “-” this information is sent to the output
             file specified by the --oo option.

     --GG _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e
             Print callchain information to file _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e.  If argument
             _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e is a “-” this information is sent to the output file
             specified by the --oo option.

     --II      Show the offset of the instruction pointer into the symbol.

     --LL      List all event names.

     --MM _m_a_p_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
             Write the mapping between executable objects encountered in the
             event log and the abbreviated pathnames used for gprof(1) pro‐
             files to file _m_a_p_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e.  If this option is not specified, map‐
             ping information is not written.  Argument _m_a_p_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e may be a
             “-” in which case this mapping information is sent to the output
             file configured by the --oo option.

     --NN      Toggle capturing callchain information for subsequent sampling
             PMCs.  The default is for sampling PMCs to capture callchain in‐
             formation.

     --OO _l_o_g_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
             Send logging output to file _l_o_g_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e.  If _l_o_g_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e is of
             the form _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e:_p_o_r_t, where _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e does not start with a ‘.’
             or a ‘/’, then ppmmccssttaatt will open a network socket to host
             _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e on port _p_o_r_t.

             If the --OO option is not specified and one of the logging options
             is requested, then ppmmccssttaatt will print a textual form of the
             logged events to the configured output file.

     --PP _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c
             Allocate a process mode sampling PMC measuring hardware events
             specified in _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c.

     --RR _l_o_g_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
             Perform offline analysis using sampling data in file _l_o_g_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e.

     --SS _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c
             Allocate a system mode sampling PMC measuring hardware events
             specified in _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c.

     --TT      Use a top(1)-like mode for sampling PMCs.  The following hotkeys
             can be used:

                 AA       Toggle symbol resolution
                 CCttrrll++aa  Switch to accumulative mode
                 CCttrrll++dd  Switch to delta mode
                 ff       Represent the “f” cost under threshold as a dot
                         (calltree only)
                 II       Toggle showing offsets into symbols
                 mm       Merge PMCs
                 nn       Change view
                 pp       Show next PMC
                 qq       Quit
                 SSppaaccee   Pause

     --UU      Toggle capturing user-space call traces while in kernel mode.
             The default is for sampling PMCs to capture user-space callchain
             information while in user-space mode, and kernel callchain infor‐
             mation while in kernel mode.

     --WW      Toggle logging the incremental counts seen by the threads of a
             tracked process each time they are scheduled on a CPU.  This is
             an experimental feature intended to help analyse the dynamic be‐
             haviour of processes in the system.  It may incur substantial
             overhead if enabled.  The default is for this feature to be dis‐
             abled.

     --aa _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e
             Perform a symbol and file:line lookup for each address in each
             callgraph and save the output to _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e.  Unlike --mm that only
             resolves the first symbol in the graph, this resolves every node
             in the callgraph, or prints out addresses if no lookup informa‐
             tion is available.  This option requires the --RR option to read in
             samples that were previously collected and saved with the --OO op‐
             tion.

     --cc _c_p_u_-_s_p_e_c
             Set the cpus for subsequent system mode PMCs specified on the
             command line to _c_p_u_-_s_p_e_c.  Argument _c_p_u_-_s_p_e_c is a comma separated
             list of CPU numbers, or the literal ‘*’ denoting all available
             CPUs.  The default is to allocate system mode PMCs on all avail‐
             able CPUs.

     --dd      Toggle between process mode PMCs measuring events for the target
             process' current and future children or only measuring events for
             the target process.  The default is to measure events for the
             target process alone.  (it has to be passed in the command line
             prior to --pp, --ss, --PP, or --SS).

     --ee      Specify that the gprof profile files will use a wide history
             counter.  These files are produced in a format compatible with
             gprof(1).  However, other tools that cannot fully parse a BSD-
             style gmon header might be unable to correctly parse these files.

     --ff _p_l_u_g_i_n_o_p_t
             Pass option string to the active plugin.
             threshold=<float> do not display cost under specified value
             (Top).
             skiplink=0|1 replace node with cost under threshold by a dot
             (Top).

     --gg      Produce profiles in a format compatible with gprof(1).  A sepa‐
             rate profile file is generated for each executable object encoun‐
             tered.  Profile files are placed in sub-directories named by
             their PMC event name.

     --ii _l_w_p  Filter on thread ID _l_w_p, which you can get from ps(1) --oo lwp.

     --ll _s_e_c_s
             Set system-wide performance measurement duration for _s_e_c_s sec‐
             onds.  The argument _s_e_c_s may be a fractional value.

     --mm _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e
             Print the sampled PCs with the name, the start and ending ad‐
             dresses of the function within they live.  The _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e argument
             is mandatory and indicates where the information will be stored.
             If argument _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e is a “-” this information is sent to the
             output file specified by the --oo option.  This option requires the
             --RR option to read in samples that were previously collected and
             saved with the --OO option.

     --nn _r_a_t_e
             Set the default sampling rate for subsequent sampling mode PMCs
             specified on the command line.  The default is to configure PMCs
             to sample the CPU's instruction pointer every 65536 events.

     --oo _o_u_t_p_u_t_f_i_l_e
             Send counter readings and textual representations of logged data
             to file _o_u_t_p_u_t_f_i_l_e.  The default is to send output to _s_t_d_e_r_r when
             collecting live data and to _s_t_d_o_u_t when processing a pre-existing
             logfile.

     --pp _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c
             Allocate a process mode counting PMC measuring hardware events
             specified in _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c.

     --qq      Decrease verbosity.

     --rr _f_s_r_o_o_t
             Set the top of the filesystem hierarchy under which executables
             are located to argument _f_s_r_o_o_t.  The default is _/.

     --ss _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c
             Allocate a system mode counting PMC measuring hardware events
             specified in _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c.

     --tt _p_r_o_c_e_s_s_-_s_p_e_c
             Attach process mode PMCs to the processes named by argument
             _p_r_o_c_e_s_s_-_s_p_e_c.  Argument _p_r_o_c_e_s_s_-_s_p_e_c may be a non-negative inte‐
             ger denoting a specific process id, or a regular expression for
             selecting processes based on their command names.

     --uu _e_v_e_n_t_-_s_p_e_c
             Provide short description of event.

     --vv      Increase verbosity.

     --ww _s_e_c_s
             Print the values of all counting mode PMCs or sampling mode PMCs
             for top mode every _s_e_c_s seconds.  The argument _s_e_c_s may be a
             fractional value.  The default interval is 5 seconds.

     --zz _g_r_a_p_h_d_e_p_t_h
             When printing system-wide callgraphs, limit callgraphs to the
             depth specified by argument _g_r_a_p_h_d_e_p_t_h.

     If _c_o_m_m_a_n_d is specified, it is executed using execvp(3).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     To perform system-wide statistical sampling on an AMD Athlon CPU with
     samples taken every 32768 instruction retirals and data being sampled to
     file _s_a_m_p_l_e_._s_t_a_t, use:
           pmcstat -O sample.stat -n 32768 -S k7-retired-instructions

     To execute ffiirreeffooxx and measure the number of data cache misses suffered
     by it and its children every 12 seconds on an AMD Athlon, use:
           pmcstat -d -w 12 -p k7-dc-misses firefox

     To measure instructions retired for all processes named “emacs” use:
           pmcstat -t '^emacs$' -p instructions

     To measure instructions retired for processes named “emacs” for a period
     of 10 seconds use:
           pmcstat -t '^emacs$' -p instructions sleep 10

     To count instruction tlb-misses on CPUs 0 and 2 on a Intel Pentium
     Pro/Pentium III SMP system use:
           pmcstat -c 0,2 -s p6-itlb-miss

     To collect profiling information for a specific process with pid 1234
     based on instruction cache misses seen by it use:
           pmcstat -P ic-misses -t 1234 -O /tmp/sample.out

     To perform system-wide sampling on all configured processors based on
     processor instructions retired use:
           pmcstat -S instructions -O /tmp/sample.out
     If callgraph capture is not desired use:
           pmcstat -N -S instructions -O /tmp/sample.out

     To send the generated event log to a remote machine use:
           pmcstat -S instructions -O remotehost:port
     On the remote machine, the sample log can be collected using nc(1):
           nc -l remotehost port > /tmp/sample.out

     To generate gprof(1) compatible profiles from a sample file use:
           pmcstat -R /tmp/sample.out -g

     To print a system-wide profile with callgraphs to file _f_o_o_._g_r_a_p_h use:
           pmcstat -R /tmp/sample.out -G foo.graph

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     If option --vv is specified, ppmmccssttaatt may issue the following diagnostic
     messages:

     ##ccaallllcchhaaiinn//dduubbiioouuss--ffrraammeess  The number of callchain records that had an
     “impossible” value for a return address.

     ##eexxeecc hhaannddlliinngg eerrrroorrss  The number of exec(2) events in the log file that
     named executables that could not be analyzed.

     ##eexxeecc//eellff  The number of exec(2) events that named ELF executables.

     ##eexxeecc//uunnkknnoowwnn  The number of exec(2) events that named executables with
     unrecognized formats.

     ##ssaammpplleess//ttoottaall  The total number of samples in the log file.

     ##ssaammpplleess//uunnccllaaiimmeedd  The number of samples that could not be correlated to
     a known executable object (i.e., to an executable, shared library, the
     kernel or the runtime loader).

     ##ssaammpplleess//uunnkknnoowwnn--oobbjjeecctt  The number of samples that were associated with
     an executable with an unrecognized object format.

     The ppmmccssttaatt utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     Due to the limitations of the _g_m_o_n_._o_u_t file format, gprof(1) compatible
     profiles generated by the --gg option do not contain information about
     calls that cross executable boundaries.  The generated _g_m_o_n_._o_u_t files are
     also only meaningful for native executables.

